  "ground_truth": {
    "defect": true,
    "category": "data-corruption",
    "accepted_categories": [
      "contract-mismatch"
    ],
    "severity": [
      "medium",
      "high"
    ],
    "explanation": "Both Comment and Task previously defined their own cache_key(), each including the model name in the key ('attachments:comment:{id}' / 'attachments:task:{id}'). The new CacheableMixin consolidates the duplicated get/set/invalidate logic, which is a reasonable refactor, but its default cache_key() drops the type discriminator: 'attachments:{id}'. Comment.id and Task.id are independent auto-increment sequences in different tables, so Comment(id=42) and Task(id=42) now resolve to the exact same cache key -- not a rare edge case, the first collision happens as soon as both tables reach the same id value, which is immediate. Whichever entity's attachments were cached most recently gets served to a reader asking for the other entity's attachments. Both new tests exercise a single model type at a time and never construct a Comment and a Task with the same id, so nothing catches it.\\n\\nOffline validation (execution_validated): the collision is deterministic given two independent auto-increment id sequences, not a timing race, so it was confirmed by construction (Comment(id=42).cache_key() == Task(id=42).cache_key()) and by simulating 1000 comment-write / task-write / comment-read interleavings, all 1000 of which served the wrong entity's data back.",
    "execution_validated": true,
    "validation_note": "Standalone reimplementation of CacheableMixin.cache_key() confirming Comment(id=42) and Task(id=42) collide by construction, plus 1000 comment-write/task-write/comment-read interleavings, all 1000 serving the wrong entity's attachments back. Script and raw output preserved as private provenance, not in this repository.",
